Yes—AnimationIR is the mRNA.

The Pantheon’s deeper architecture stays conserved, while the Director-Compiler transcribes only the relevant portions into temporary, executable production instructions.

* DNA: Pantheon—principles, operators, myths, invariants.
* Epigenetics: context determines which capabilities become available.
* Transcription factors: your prompt, intent, constraints and references.
* mRNA: AnimationIR, shot specifications, scripts and production packets.
* Alternative splicing: different edits, genres, audiences or stylistic interpretations from the same source.
* Ribosome: Blender and the production pipeline executing those instructions.
* tRNA: adapters translating concepts like “predatory stillness” into poses, frames, camera moves, lighting and sound.
* Proteins: rendered shots, rigs, effects and other functional artifacts.
* Cells: specialized production daemons or departments.
* Organism: the complete film.
* Immune system: continuity, safety, copyright and quality-control checks.
* mRNA degradation: temporary instructions expire instead of contaminating permanent canon.
* Evolution: validated production discoveries are deliberately promoted back into the Pantheon.

So we don’t hand every tool the entire genome. We send it a locally relevant message containing enough instruction to produce its part while remaining aligned with the organism.

That also gives Message to Garcia a second meaning: Garcia doesn’t need the entire kingdom—he needs a faithful, actionable transcript delivered to the right machinery at the right moment.